WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.223
This is Chapter 6: group and
delegated managed service accounts,

4
00:00:10.223 --> 00:00:11.850
and the Kerberos island.

5
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.679
These accounts are good credentials
with a known expiry date,

6
00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:20.108
the day the last domain controller is
demoted, so the chapter makes sure

7
00:00:20.108 --> 00:00:24.162
every one of them moves, retires, or
sits on a dated list before then.

8
00:00:24.162 --> 00:00:28.258
We'll start from Learn's own
procedures, see what they leave out,

9
00:00:28.258 --> 00:00:33.075
then walk the plan, the island list, the
write path, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:38.580
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.743
Why does this chapter have
an end date built in?

12
00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:49.354
Because a group managed service account
only works while A D is in charge.

13
00:00:49.354 --> 00:00:54.363
The program decides early whether A D is
retired or kept as a dated Kerberos island,

14
00:00:54.363 --> 00:00:58.414
and the risk register is blunt:
anything still running under one of

15
00:00:58.414 --> 00:01:02.980
these accounts loses its credential
when the last domain controller goes.

16
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.292
So converting isn't the finish line.

17
00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:09.139
Every account must end up
moving to a workload identity,

18
00:01:09.139 --> 00:01:11.480
retiring, or on a dated island list.

19
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:15.855
The program adds rules of its
own, labeled as design guidance.

20
00:01:15.855 --> 00:01:18.934
Each account's disposition
isn't decided here:

21
00:01:18.934 --> 00:01:22.743
it's the owner's decision from
Chapter 3, read from that run.

22
00:01:22.743 --> 00:01:26.160
And the strictest rule
is simple: never delete.

23
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.385
A quick grounding in
what these accounts are.

24
00:01:32.385 --> 00:01:36.396
A group managed service account is
a domain account whose password the

25
00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:40.610
domain controllers manage and rotate,
and which several servers can fetch.

26
00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:45.101
Learn adds rules the plan respects:
its name must be unique in the forest,

27
00:01:45.101 --> 00:01:48.526
its password interval can be
set only when it's created,

28
00:01:48.526 --> 00:01:52.788
and a forest should have only one
key distribution service root key,

29
00:01:52.788 --> 00:01:56.898
because more than one makes these
accounts fail after a rotation.

30
00:01:56.898 --> 00:02:00.115
The delegated kind is new
in Windows Server 2025:

31
00:02:00.115 --> 00:02:05.102
it takes over from an old-style service
account, and once the move completes,

32
00:02:05.102 --> 00:02:09.285
the old password stops working
and the old account is disabled.

33
00:02:09.285 --> 00:02:13.760
The workbook's migration path matrix
says which target each account gets.

34
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.235
And because everything here is an Active
Directory command against the domain,

35
00:02:18.235 --> 00:02:21.780
the cloud choice doesn't change
anything in this chapter.

36
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.383
Here's what Microsoft
gives you: four procedures,

37
00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:32.805
covering the delegated account
walk-through, its limits, the group account

38
00:02:32.805 --> 00:02:37.655
walk-through, and the one-time root key that
every managed password is derived from.

39
00:02:37.655 --> 00:02:42.395
These procedures live only inside web
pages, so the repository pins their

40
00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:47.292
source unmodified, with hashes a test
checks and a weekly watch for changes.

41
00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:51.040
Then a harness runs each PowerShell
block exactly as published,

42
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.354
against stand-ins that accept only
the parameters the real commands have.

43
00:02:55.354 --> 00:02:58.970
That last detail matters,
as you'll see in a moment.

44
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.207
This is where the
stand-ins earn their keep.

45
00:03:05.207 --> 00:03:09.358
The creation steps are fine, and
the code keeps them as its model.

46
00:03:09.358 --> 00:03:13.366
But the root key check uses a
command that returns the key itself,

47
00:03:13.366 --> 00:03:18.508
the secret every managed password comes
from, so the code reads only its start time.

48
00:03:18.508 --> 00:03:22.421
The backdated key is meant for
a test lab, not production.

49
00:03:22.421 --> 00:03:25.647
And one step sets the migration
state with a parameter

50
00:03:25.647 --> 00:03:28.945
the command simply doesn't
have, so as published it fails.

51
00:03:28.945 --> 00:03:31.341
Placeholder names that change from step to

52
00:03:31.341 --> 00:03:34.220
step are replaced by
names from reviewed data.

53
00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.820
The rest of the gaps are
about timing and safety.

54
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:45.157
The walk-through completes a migration
with no wait, while the overview

55
00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:49.420
asks for weeks, so the code waits thirty
days, the program's parallel run.

56
00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.213
It never checks the limits, like
unconstrained delegation that

57
00:03:53.213 --> 00:03:57.220
silently stops working, so the code
checks each one before planning.

58
00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.870
It restarts every service on the host.

59
00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.002
And the pages disagree on
deleting the old account;

60
00:04:03.002 --> 00:04:06.432
the code takes the stricter
side and has no delete at all.

61
00:04:06.432 --> 00:04:09.690
So the verdict: use Learn's
commands and their order,

62
00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.440
but not the procedures as the program.

63
00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.515
So what does the code add?

64
00:04:17.515 --> 00:04:20.684
A module, two entry scripts
kept strictly apart,

65
00:04:20.684 --> 00:04:25.478
one that only reads and one that writes,
and its own list of allowed calls.

66
00:04:25.478 --> 00:04:29.599
It builds on Chapter 1's register
and on Chapter 3's decisions,

67
00:04:29.599 --> 00:04:32.566
and refuses either if
its hash doesn't match.

68
00:04:32.566 --> 00:04:36.173
The owner's decision for each
account comes from Chapter 3,

69
00:04:36.173 --> 00:04:37.677
never from a second list.

70
00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:40.431
Then three reviewed
files: the new account,

71
00:04:40.431 --> 00:04:44.993
workload and hosts for each account
being re-platformed, who may fetch

72
00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:49.791
each managed password, and the one
program dates file Chapter 3 also reads.

73
00:04:49.791 --> 00:04:52.228
Notice that the end state has no default.

74
00:04:52.228 --> 00:04:56.690
If the decision is missing, the run
simply stops rather than guessing.

75
00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.153
The reader collects the
root key's start time,

76
00:05:03.153 --> 00:05:07.845
every managed account with its password
retrievers and migration state, and,

77
00:05:07.845 --> 00:05:12.977
for a delegated conversion, the domain
controllers and each host's operating system.

78
00:05:12.977 --> 00:05:14.428
Then nine rules run.

79
00:05:14.428 --> 00:05:19.292
Some come from Learn: the root key must
exist and be old enough, the migration's

80
00:05:19.292 --> 00:05:24.002
limits must hold, and managed accounts
shouldn't sit in privileged groups.

81
00:05:24.002 --> 00:05:28.057
Others come from the project plan:
every account needs a decision,

82
00:05:28.057 --> 00:05:30.610
an owner for its service principal names,

83
00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:35.040
an end date inside the program's
deadline, and a reconciled destination.

84
00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.510
Every finding gets a stable ID.

85
00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.872
Each account becomes exactly one action.

86
00:05:43.872 --> 00:05:48.646
The group account path is: create it,
wait while the server owner installs it,

87
00:05:48.646 --> 00:05:51.341
confirms each host can fetch its password,

88
00:05:51.341 --> 00:05:55.498
moves the service principal names
and switches the service over,

89
00:05:55.498 --> 00:05:59.348
run in parallel for thirty days,
then disable the old account.

90
00:05:59.348 --> 00:06:04.389
The delegated path is: create it, start
the migration, wait the full thirty days,

91
00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:07.541
then complete it, which
disables the old account

92
00:06:07.541 --> 00:06:10.535
and moves its service
principal names across.

93
00:06:10.535 --> 00:06:12.885
Anything else is recorded with a reason.

94
00:06:12.885 --> 00:06:16.646
And because the same inputs
always produce the same file,

95
00:06:16.646 --> 00:06:19.810
byte for byte, a reviewer
approves one hash.

96
00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.122
Two outputs carry the chapter's end date.

97
00:06:26.122 --> 00:06:29.794
The island list holds every
account that will stay on Kerberos,

98
00:06:29.794 --> 00:06:34.172
under the name it will end up with, along
with its disposition from Chapter 3,

99
00:06:34.172 --> 00:06:36.996
its owner, its end date and
the date that bounds it,

100
00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:40.597
and whether it's ready to hand to
the program's retirement work.

101
00:06:40.597 --> 00:06:43.045
An account kept on Kerberos is ready only

102
00:06:43.045 --> 00:06:45.709
with the authorizing
official's acceptance.

103
00:06:45.709 --> 00:06:50.210
The hand-off flag only turns true
when every single row is ready.

104
00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.506
The reconciliation covers
every managed account,

105
00:06:53.506 --> 00:06:56.410
existing or planned, and
says where it ends up.

106
00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:59.677
When the program confirms
its end-state decision,

107
00:06:59.677 --> 00:07:03.502
the program dates file changes
in a reviewed pull request,

108
00:07:03.502 --> 00:07:08.230
and Chapter 3's run and this one are
repeated well before A D retires.

109
00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:14.591
Writing to the domain
is the dangerous part,

110
00:07:14.591 --> 00:07:19.305
so it lives in its own script that refuses
to run unless several things are true.

111
00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:23.255
The plan must be the exact one
a reviewer approved, by hash.

112
00:07:23.255 --> 00:07:26.255
It must be for this domain
and less than a week old.

113
00:07:26.255 --> 00:07:30.793
Every write must be a declared call
with only its allowed parameters,

114
00:07:30.793 --> 00:07:35.330
and nothing that deletes, uninstalls,
undoes or resets exists to call.

115
00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:37.843
It runs as a person, never a job.

116
00:07:37.843 --> 00:07:40.577
Then, before each write,
it checks the target

117
00:07:40.577 --> 00:07:44.200
again and skips anything
that changed since review.

118
00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:49.000
Who's involved?

119
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.738
Developers build the code.

120
00:07:50.738 --> 00:07:55.212
Server and directory engineers run
the write path and change the hosts.

121
00:07:55.212 --> 00:08:00.148
Application owners name who owns each
service principal name and when it ends,

122
00:08:00.148 --> 00:08:03.262
and the security officer
reviews the island list.

123
00:08:03.262 --> 00:08:05.563
The rights split is the important part.

124
00:08:05.563 --> 00:08:09.060
The read task holds no domain
administration at all,

125
00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:12.475
just a narrow delegated read
of the root key's dates.

126
00:08:12.475 --> 00:08:15.347
The write task does need
administrator rights,

127
00:08:15.347 --> 00:08:19.397
because Learn's procedure requires
them, and that's exactly why it's

128
00:08:19.397 --> 00:08:23.650
a separate script, run by a person,
on a plan someone else approved.

129
00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:33.672
Lab 1 needs only a laptop, PowerShell 7
and Pester, plus the runs of Chapters 1,

130
00:08:33.672 --> 00:08:38.325
2 and 3: the register, the owners
and the owners' decisions.

131
00:08:38.325 --> 00:08:42.491
First you run Learn's own procedures
against the stand-ins, and you can read

132
00:08:42.491 --> 00:08:46.862
the test where the migration-state step
fails, just as it would on a real server.

133
00:08:46.862 --> 00:08:51.974
Then you list what each task is allowed
to run: the read task is only lookups.

134
00:08:51.974 --> 00:08:54.650
Then you run the check
against a fictional estate.

135
00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.616
Nine accounts are in
scope, with five findings,

136
00:08:57.616 --> 00:09:01.050
four planned writes, and a
hand-off that isn't ready yet.

137
00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:02.450
Let's see why.

138
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.425
Reading the findings is where
the rules become concrete.

139
00:09:09.425 --> 00:09:12.875
Every decision here is the
owner's, from Chapter 3.

140
00:09:12.875 --> 00:09:16.948
One web server account is trusted
for unconstrained delegation,

141
00:09:16.948 --> 00:09:22.002
which stops working after a delegated
migration, so its owner kept it on Kerberos,

142
00:09:22.002 --> 00:09:26.150
with the authorizing official's
acceptance, but it has no S P N owner.

143
00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:28.647
Another account's
reviewed retrievers add a

144
00:09:28.647 --> 00:09:31.212
server that Active
Directory doesn't have yet.

145
00:09:31.212 --> 00:09:34.675
An intranet account being
re-platformed has no owner

146
00:09:34.675 --> 00:09:37.824
and an end date years out,
so it can't be reconciled.

147
00:09:37.824 --> 00:09:39.850
One sits in Domain Admins.

148
00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:44.067
Meanwhile the plan completes a
migration that started forty days ago,

149
00:09:44.067 --> 00:09:46.530
and creates two new group accounts.

150
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.280
Next you read the island list and
the reconciliation side by side.

151
00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:57.693
Each row now carries its
disposition from Chapter 3.

152
00:09:57.693 --> 00:10:01.418
Two rows aren't ready for
hand-off, so the flag stays false.

153
00:10:01.418 --> 00:10:05.521
The web front end carries the
authorizing official's acceptance,

154
00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:08.181
so it becomes ready once
it has an S P N owner.

155
00:10:08.181 --> 00:10:12.568
That's the point: which is the point:
an incomplete list never goes.

156
00:10:12.568 --> 00:10:13.892
Then the write path.

157
00:10:13.892 --> 00:10:18.106
A dry run with the approved hash
reports the four writes it would send.

158
00:10:18.106 --> 00:10:22.170
Applying it to the fake sends them,
and the results tell the operator

159
00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:26.017
what comes next, such as installing
each new account on its hosts.

160
00:10:26.017 --> 00:10:30.281
Finally you pass a hash nobody
approved, and it refuses outright.

161
00:10:30.281 --> 00:10:34.040
The full suite is five hundred
thirteen passing tests.

162
00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:41.110
Lab 2 moves to a test
domain, never production,

163
00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:45.865
with a Windows Server 2025 domain
controller and member server.

164
00:10:45.865 --> 00:10:48.604
You make two test accounts,
one for each path,

165
00:10:48.604 --> 00:10:52.408
record their decisions by running
Chapters 2 and 3 over them,

166
00:10:52.408 --> 00:10:56.897
and grant the reader only a narrow
delegated read of the root key's dates,

167
00:10:56.897 --> 00:11:00.853
recording whether that really
works without exposing the key.

168
00:11:00.853 --> 00:11:04.291
A second person reviews the
plan and hands you its hash.

169
00:11:04.291 --> 00:11:08.715
You apply it as a person, then run
the next plan to start the migration,

170
00:11:08.715 --> 00:11:12.665
restart only the test service,
and watch the Kerberos events.

171
00:11:12.665 --> 00:11:16.610
After thirty days, complete
it, or back it out by hand.

172
00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.735
Each of these failures
has a guard in the code.

173
00:11:22.735 --> 00:11:25.857
The biggest is the one the
chapter is built around:

174
00:11:25.857 --> 00:11:30.578
an account left behind when A D retires,
and the guard is the reconciliation,

175
00:11:30.578 --> 00:11:33.547
rerun whenever the
end-state decision changes.

176
00:11:33.547 --> 00:11:38.162
Others are about the migration itself:
completing too early, on a host that

177
00:11:38.162 --> 00:11:42.398
can't use the new account, or deleting
the old one so there's no way back.

178
00:11:42.398 --> 00:11:46.020
Then the quieter ones: reading
the root key as a secret,

179
00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:50.384
widening retrievers by hand, or
a plan that changed after review.

180
00:11:50.384 --> 00:11:53.420
Each guard is a rule that runs every time.

181
00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:58.520
Here's what done looks like.

182
00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.595
Every Kerberos account
has a reviewed decision.

183
00:12:01.595 --> 00:12:05.383
The root key was checked without
anyone holding administrator rights.

184
00:12:05.383 --> 00:12:10.467
Retrievers match the reviewed list, no
migration broke the limits or finished early,

185
00:12:10.467 --> 00:12:14.970
and every plan was approved by someone
other than the person who applied it.

186
00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:16.220
Nothing was deleted.

187
00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:20.392
Every island account has an owner
and an end date inside the deadline,

188
00:12:20.392 --> 00:12:23.832
and every managed account
has a reconciled destination.

189
00:12:23.832 --> 00:12:26.301
With the Kerberos accounts accounted for,

190
00:12:26.301 --> 00:12:29.757
Chapter 7 turns to least
privilege for service principals.

191
00:12:29.757 --> 00:12:30.910
Thanks for watching.
